0.0,-4.005352093372494e-05,-6.079655122448457e-06,-2.622600959512056e-06],"request":{"active_prefix":"","input_ids":[151644,8948,198,2610,525,1207,16948,11,3465,553,54364,14817,13,1446,525,264,10950,17847,13,151645,198,151644,872,198,50,3948,279,3491,3019,553,3019,13,20678,697,1590,4226,304,1124,79075,90,1112,92,382,46254,198,59,26056,1242,15159,16,1124,273,502,366,600,92,1124,37018,90,16,15170,17,47822,72,488,502,38154,59,60,2870,279,2629,374,4429,916,678,6785,25780,400,72,3,323,400,73,3,1741,429,400,16,1124,273,502,366,600,2418,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nCompute\n\\[\\sum_{1 \\le j < i} \\frac{1}{2^{i + j}},\\]where the sum is taken over all positive integers $i$ and $j$ such that $1 \\le j < i.$","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"a1e480bf649c245cf1ae35df0f7604cca1f570f5a9bfd8d8f151b6c15b122cfe"},"seconds":58.38505983352661,"seed":781628971975963418,"stop":"eos","text":"To compute the sum\n\\[\n\\sum_{1 \\le j < i} \\frac{1}{2^{i + j}},\n\\]\nwe start by examining the sum's structure. The condition \\(1 \\le j < i\\) means that for each \\(i\\), \\(j\\) ranges from 1 to \\(i-1\\).\n\nWe can rewrite the sum as\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=2}^{\\infty} \\sum_{j=1}^{i-1} \\frac{1}{2^{i + j}}.\n\\]\nHere, the outer sum is over all \\(i \\geq 2\\) because \\(j\\) must be less than \\(i\\), and the inner sum is over all \\(j\\) from 1 to \\(i-1\\).\n\nNext, we can simplify the inner sum. For a fixed \\(i\\),\n\\[\n\\sum_{j=1}^{i-1} \\frac{1}{2^{i + j}} = \\frac{1}{2^i} \\sum_{j=1}^{i-1} \\frac{1}{2^j}.\n\\]\nThe sum \\(\\sum_{j=1}^{i-1} \\frac{1}{2^j}\\) is a geometric series with the first term \\(\\frac{1}{2}\\) and common ratio \\(\\frac{1}{2}\\). The sum of the first \\(i-1\\) terms of this series is\n\\[\n\\sum_{j=1}^{i-1} \\frac{1}{2^j} = \\frac{\\frac{1}{2} (1 - (\\frac{1}{2})^{i-1})}{1 - \\frac{1}{2}} = 1 - \\frac{1}{2^{i-1}}.\n\\]\nTherefore,\n\\[\n\\sum_{j=1}^{i-1} \\frac{1}{2^{i + j}} = \\frac{1}{2^i} \\left(1 - \\frac{1}{2^{i-1}}\\right) = \\frac{1}{2^i} - \\frac{1}{2^{2i-1}}.\n\\]\n\nNow we substitute this back into the outer sum:\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=2}^{\\infty} \\left( \\frac{1}{2^i} - \\frac{1}{2^{2i-1}} \\right).\n\\]\nWe can split this into two separate sums:\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=2}^{\\infty} \\frac{1}{2^i} - \\sum_{i=2}^{\\infty} \\frac{1}{2^{2i-1}}.\n\\]\n\nFirst, consider the sum \\(\\sum_{i=2}^{\\infty} \\frac{1}{2^i}\\). This is a geometric series starting from \\(i=2\\):\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=2}^{\\infty} \\frac{1}{2^i} = \\frac{1}{2^2} + \\frac{1}{2^3} + \\frac{1}{2^4} + \\cdots = \\frac{\\frac{1}{4}}{1 - \\frac{1}{2}} = \\frac{1}{2}.\n\\]\n\nNext, consider the sum \\(\\sum_{i=2}^{\\infty} \\frac{1}{2^{2i-1}}\\). This is also a geometric series:\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=2}^{\\infty} \\frac{1}{2^{2i-1}} = \\frac{1}{2^3} + \\frac{1}{2^5} + \\frac{1}{2^7} + \\cdots = \\frac{\\frac{1}{8}}{1 - \\frac{1}{4}} = \\frac{\\frac{1}{8}}{\\frac{3}{4}} = \\frac{1}{6}.\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the original sum is\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{1}{6} = \\frac{3}{6} - \\frac{1}{6} = \\frac{2}{6} = \\frac{1}{3}.\n\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{1}{3}}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{\\frac{1}{3}}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
